From 0637f4af0d1497783bf802adc8c561daf1b146e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dixonSolutions <203963471+dixonSolutions@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 08:04:06 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 136/152] Night Light through the HX83102E digital gamma

The DPU's only colour hardware (the VGRF HDR block) garbled the picture
and once faulted the panel. The panel itself has per-channel digital
gamma: Samsung's init sequence loads one 46-point 10-bit curve per colour
(0xC1 in banks 1-3 behind the 0xB9 password). Measured on the device:
sending Samsung's green and blue curves scaled by 0.75 and 0.5 over DCS
gave a clean warm tint, and restoring them gave the original picture.

- drm: exynos9810: back to scanning out from IDMA_G0 only; with
  samsung,panel-gamma the CRTC gets a 256-entry gamma LUT whose
  per-channel top entries (exact for GNOME's linear Night Light ramps)
  go to a panel gamma hook (soc/samsung/exynos9810-panel-gamma.h)
- backlight: exynos9810-dsim: with samsung,hx83102e-dgc, register as that
  hook and load the scaled curves from a work item that only sends the
  newest request (Night Light fades in over many commits). The packing
  reproduces Samsung's three tables byte for byte (checked on the host).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
